"For more than two centuries before 221 BCE, China was divided among rival kingdoms, a time known as the Warring States. Each kingdom had its own coins, its own weights and measures, and its own laws. A trader from Qi in the east paid with heavy bronze knives; in Chu in the south, people used small coins shaped like shells. Between 230 and 221 BCE, the western kingdom of Qin conquered the other six, one after another.": "c2bf702b088f",
"For more than two centuries before 221 BCE, China was divided among rival kingdoms, a time known as the Warring States. Each kingdom had its own coins, its own weights and measures, and its own laws. A trader from Qi in the east paid with heavy bronze knives; in Chu in the south, people used small coins shaped like shells. Between 230 and 221 BCE, the western kingdom of Qin conquered the other six, one after another. The king of Qin took a new title, Huangdi, “emperor,” and is remembered as the First Emperor. His government ordered one standard for laws, weights and measures, the width of cart axles, and writing. An old Qin measuring cup in the Shanghai Museum, made in 344 BCE, also carries the edict of 221 BCE. The round Qin coin with a square hole became the only legal bronze coin, and that shape was used in East Asia for about 2,000 years. The Qin also built roads, including the Straight Road toward the northern frontier, and joined sections of earlier walls into one line of defense. All this took the forced labor of huge numbers of people. Qin law was strict, and in 213 BCE the government ordered private copies of many books to be burned, sparing books on medicine, divination, and farming. The next year, the historian Sima Qian writes, more than 460 scholars were put to death. The Qin dynasty did not last. Soon after the First Emperor died in 210 BCE, rebellions broke out, and by 207–206 BCE the Qin had fallen. The Han dynasty that followed lasted, with one interruption, until 220 CE. The Han kept many Qin standards and its centralized government, while turning toward Confucian ideas of moderate rule. Emperor Wu appointed scholars for the Confucian classics and asked each region to recommend honest men for office. In 118 BCE he introduced the wuzhu coin, used for centuries afterward. The Han empire also reached out to the world. Around 138 BCE, the envoy Zhang Qian set out west and returned about thirteen years later with news of distant lands; Lesson 9 follows the roads he opened. A census recorded in the History of the Former Han counts almost 60 million people. In 105 CE, an official named Cai Lun presented a new way of making paper from bark, hemp, rags, and old fishing nets; archaeologists have also found older pieces of paper. At the same time, at the other end of Eurasia, the Roman Empire also ruled tens of millions of people. Both empires built roads, made laws, and issued coins. Among their differences, Rome spread its citizenship, while the Han relied on a shared script and on officials chosen for their learning and good name. Each empire knew very little about the other. Comparing them shows two different ways of holding a huge land together, each with its own costs.": "7027d88394f7",
